1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,263
Do you want to become a DevOps engineer, but you have no idea where to start?

2
00:00:04,733 --> 00:00:07,150
Welcome to the DevOps Engineering Bootcamp.

3
00:00:07,170 --> 00:00:11,061
A step by step course that takes you from zero to building, securing,

4
00:00:11,081 --> 00:00:12,478
and running real systems.

5
00:00:17,998 --> 00:00:20,645
Here is how we teach every tool. Five steps.

6
00:00:21,115 --> 00:00:23,847
Learn it: understand the idea, and why it exists.

7
00:00:26,817 --> 00:00:29,399
Build it: set it up yourself, in a working lab.

8
00:00:32,369 --> 00:00:34,931
Break it: watch what fails, in a safe lab.

9
00:00:37,901 --> 00:00:40,185
Fix it: find the cause, and repair it.

10
00:00:43,155 --> 00:00:46,143
And operate it: secure it, monitor it, and keep it running.

11
00:00:50,663 --> 00:00:54,846
The course is planned in nine stages, and each stage builds on the one before.

12
00:00:55,316 --> 00:00:59,872
Foundations: Linux, Bash, Python, and Git. Then applications and build tools:

13
00:00:59,892 --> 00:01:01,952
Java, Maven, Tomcat, and databases.

14
00:01:05,422 --> 00:01:10,372
Then C I C D with Jenkins, SonarQube, and Nexus, and containers with Docker.

15
00:01:12,842 --> 00:01:16,035
Then the cloud, Terraform and Ansible, and Kubernetes.

16
00:01:16,055 --> 00:01:20,140
Then security, monitoring, reliability, and a final capstone project.

17
00:01:23,610 --> 00:01:24,475
This is the plan.

18
00:01:24,495 --> 00:01:28,539
Lessons are published stage by stage, in order, so each one has what it needs.

19
00:01:33,059 --> 00:01:35,364
Every lesson follows the same shape.

20
00:01:35,834 --> 00:01:39,932
First, why: the problem the tool solves. Then the concept, in plain words.

21
00:01:42,902 --> 00:01:47,383
Then a demonstration, with real commands and real output. No made up results.

22
00:01:50,853 --> 00:01:52,614
Then practice, which you do yourself,

23
00:01:52,634 --> 00:01:54,972
and a checker that tells you if you got it right.

24
00:01:59,492 --> 00:02:01,947
A few promises about what to expect.

25
00:02:02,417 --> 00:02:03,593
No experience needed.

26
00:02:03,613 --> 00:02:07,239
We explain every new term and command the first time it appears.

27
00:02:09,709 --> 00:02:13,815
Linux lessons use Ubuntu, a free system you can run on your own computer,

28
00:02:13,835 --> 00:02:15,001
in a virtual machine.

29
00:02:17,471 --> 00:02:21,825
When something is an illustration instead of a real capture, we label it on screen.

30
00:02:24,295 --> 00:02:27,315
Some cloud lessons may need an account with a cloud provider.

31
00:02:27,335 --> 00:02:29,907
We will tell you the cost before you click anything.

32
00:02:32,877 --> 00:02:34,315
We cannot promise you a job.

33
00:02:34,335 --> 00:02:37,806
We can promise real practice, and a way to check your own progress.

34
00:02:42,326 --> 00:02:45,564
Start with lesson one: the software development life cycle.

35
00:02:45,584 --> 00:02:48,215
How does an idea become something customers use?

36
00:02:51,685 --> 00:02:55,335
Subscribe, and follow the playlist in order. See you in lesson one.
